    {"problem": "The back bedrooms get only a trickle from the vents", "solution": "Many pre-1960 houses have small round ducts and a single central return, a layout left over from gravity furnaces. We test static pressure, check each run, and then add returns, upsize the worst trunk sections or bring in a ductless head for the rooms that never get enough air."},
    {"problem": "A new furnace went in and the vents started whistling", "solution": "A modern variable-speed blower pushing into undersized ducts gets loud at the registers and works harder than it should. We set blower speeds to what your ductwork can actually carry, and we size the furnace from a heat-loss number instead of copying the old nameplate."},
    {"problem": "The stone walls feel cold no matter what the thermostat says", "solution": "Solid masonry soaks up heat slowly and gives it back slowly. We size for the walls you really have, and in many Galt homes a ductless head on an inside wall gives steady heat where the furnace struggles to reach."},
    {"problem": "The finished attic is unbearable from June to September", "solution": "Attic rooms in older brick homes often have one small supply duct or none. A single-zone ductless heat pump cools that floor in summer and adds efficient heat in winter, with only a small opening for the refrigerant lines."},
