  {"type": "split", "photo": "in", "reverse": True,
   "h2": "Heat pump installation in Cambridge when the ducts are the question",
   "html": "<p>In an older Cambridge house, the first question isn't which brand of heat pump to buy. It's whether the ducts can carry what a heat pump needs. A heat pump delivers a steadier, gentler stream of air than a furnace, and it needs roughly 400 cubic feet per minute of airflow for every ton of capacity. Ducts sized decades ago for a hot-air furnace often come up short.</p>"
           "<p>So we measure. A static pressure test shows how hard the blower is working against the ducts. If the reading is reasonable, a ducted cold-climate heat pump, or a hybrid system with a high-efficiency furnace, can go on the existing ductwork. If it isn't, we either fix the worst sections or split the job: ducted heat for the main floor and ductless heads for the rooms the ducts can't serve.</p>"
           "<p>In stone and double-brick homes with no ducts at all, ductless is usually the cleanest answer. Each indoor head needs a short refrigerant line through the wall, and we core that opening neatly and seal it so water stays out of the masonry.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Static pressure and airflow test on your existing ducts</li>"
           "<li>Manual J heat-loss calculation, room by room</li>"
           "<li>Ducted, ductless or a mix, priced side by side</li>"
           "<li>Masonry openings cored, sleeved and sealed</li></ul>"},
